#!/sbin/openrc-run
# infra-os:   alpine
# infra-init: openrc
# infra-path: /etc/init.d
# infra-name: cloudflared
# infra-mode: 0755
#
# HAND-WRITTEN, AND IT HAS TO BE. Alpine packages no init script for cloudflared —
# docs/host-setup.md installs the binary from GitHub — so this was assembled by hand.
# Whatever preceded it DID NOT START ON REBOOT: `cloudflared service install` produces
# something that looks correct, starts when started, and is missing the next time the
# box comes up. Do not replace this file with it.
#
# FIRST TRACKED /etc FILE ON `two`. It only became trackable on 2026-08-23, when the
# token moved out of it — before that, committing this file would have committed a
# secret, which is why zero and one were tracked in August and this was not.
#
# NO log_proxy ON THIS HOST. zero and one pipe through it for rotation, but it arrived
# there with docker and two runs neither docker nor containerd. So this uses OpenRC's
# plain file variables: output_log and error_log map to --stdout and --stderr, per
# /usr/libexec/rc/sh/supervise-daemon.sh on this box. `stdout_log`/`stderr_log`, which
# this file used to declare, are read by nothing — /var/log/cloudflared.err was frozen
# at 1 MB since 16 July and cloudflared.log empty since 26 December.
#
# The cost of no log_proxy is no rotation. 1 MB in seven months, on 27 GB free, is slow
# enough to leave; if it ever matters, logrotate is a smaller change than a package.

# NO AUTOUPDATE, since 2026-08-23. It was `--autoupdate-freq 24h0m0s` and it was live:
# all three binaries were rewritten on 14-15 August, the day 2026.8.2 was released, on a
# fleet where cloudflared was installed by hand half a year earlier.
#
# Two reasons it is off. First, it replaced a boot-path binary with unverified bytes on a
# 24-hour cycle — the same thing dev/Dockerfile refuses to permit inside a container, on
# the grounds that this binary "holds the credentials that put an ssh endpoint on the
# public internet", and the argument is stronger here, not weaker.
#
# Second, it could never fix what is wrong: cloudflared updates itself with a build
# matching ITS OWN architecture, not the machine's. All three hosts carry byte-identical
# binaries though `two` is armv6 and the others arm64 — so the fleet has been running one
# 32-bit build everywhere since install, and autoupdate perpetuated that indefinitely.
#
# Updates now come from ansible/playbooks/cloudflared-update.yml: right arch per host,
# hash-verified, one host at a time, one -> two -> zero, each stage refusing while an
# earlier one is unhealthy.

cfgfile="/etc/$RC_SVCNAME/$RC_SVCNAME.conf"
supervisor=supervise-daemon
command="/usr/bin/cloudflared"
command_args="--no-autoupdate --config /etc/cloudflared/config.yml tunnel run"
command_user="root"
pidfile="/var/run/$RC_SVCNAME.pid"
command_background="yes"

# `cfgfile` and `command_background` both look vestigial — nothing references either.
# Left alone rather than removed alongside the changes that had to happen here.

# Both streams to one file: cloudflared writes essentially everything to stderr, which
# is why the old split left .log empty for eight months and .err at 1 MB.
output_log="/var/log/$RC_SVCNAME.log"
error_log="/var/log/$RC_SVCNAME.log"

# 0640, matching zero and one. Without log_proxy there is no LOGPROXY_CHMOD, so the file
# is pre-created at the right mode instead — the pattern /etc/init.d/containerd uses on
# the other boxes. This service has twice left a world-readable file containing its own
# credential on this fleet; its log does not get world-read by default either.
start_pre() {
	checkpath -f -m 0640 -o root:root "/var/log/$RC_SVCNAME.log"
}

depend() {
	need net
}
